{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794340584,"nonce":"f/N33ypVh4FBT3/A8tniCCOvPaI=","title":"perf: a keystroke waits for the next frame callback while frames run continuously","message":"Typing into a text_input does not rebuild the tree: the key handler bumps the field's revision signal, the bound runs and the caret transform update, and the frame lays out and presents. Release build, Wayland (kwin virtual), 28 px field, German layout, probe printing key press to end of queue submit:\n\n- Idle app (no animation running): 0.85 to 1.3 ms from key to present across about 45 key groups, including 120 chars into one field. Stages: prop updates 0.1 to 0.2 ms, layout 0.2 to 0.4 ms, present at about 1 ms. No full rebuild on any key.\n- Same app in a run where frames were presented continuously at 60 Hz (cause not found; seen in 1 of 5 runs): key to present was 2.4 to 17.8 ms, evenly spread. The key is received at once, but the frame is only started on the next compositor frame callback; the work after it is still about 1 ms.\n\nSo the target of under 8 ms holds whenever the app is idle, and is missed on average (about 9 ms) when something keeps frames running, by the vsync wait alone. Fixing that means rendering the key's frame without waiting for the frame callback, or finding what keeps frames running and stopping it. Both are changes to the frame loop, not a small fix.\n\nOpen: what kept the app at 60 Hz in that run (a focused empty field and a pointer over the input were the only state).\n\nProbe: key stamp in the platform key handler, present stamp after frame.present(), stage stamps around prop updates and layout. Local only.","files":null}]}